WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.190
- Alright y'all we're back,

00:00:01.190 --> 00:00:03.195
and I'm hanging with Kyle MacLachlan.

00:00:03.195 --> 00:00:05.770
(audience applauds)

00:00:05.770 --> 00:00:08.150
- Alright everybody, our next
guest is a musical legend

00:00:08.150 --> 00:00:10.480
who's been at it on his
own and with his band,

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:14.020
Creedence Clearwater Revival
for over five decades.

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:17.010
His new song is called
"Weeping In The Promised Land"

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:18.320
What an amazing title.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:19.710
And it's out right now.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:21.890
Please welcome Rock and Roll Hall of Famer

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:23.750
and songwriting Hall of Famer,

00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:25.399
John Fogerty.

00:00:25.399 --> 00:00:27.265
(lively upbeat music)

00:00:27.265 --> 00:00:30.348
( audience applauds)

00:00:42.663 --> 00:00:43.632
I wish I could hug you.

00:00:43.632 --> 00:00:44.831
- Hello hello.

00:00:44.831 --> 00:00:46.923
- Go ahead, have a seat.

00:00:46.923 --> 00:00:47.756
- Thank you

00:00:47.756 --> 00:00:48.805
- So meet Kyle.

00:00:48.805 --> 00:00:49.638
- Hi John.

00:00:49.638 --> 00:00:50.690
- Hi Kyle.
What a pleasure.

00:00:50.690 --> 00:00:53.740
- The last time we met,
it was at Yankee stadium?

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:54.573
- That's true.

00:00:54.573 --> 00:00:57.111
- Yeah. It was a sports ball
event, which I never am at.

00:00:57.111 --> 00:00:57.944
(laughs)

00:00:57.944 --> 00:00:59.880
- Well, you're missing some of that there.

00:00:59.880 --> 00:01:03.790
It was the opening day of
the brand new Yankee Stadium.

00:01:03.790 --> 00:01:06.550
The Yankees went on that
year to win the World Series.

00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:08.210
- I think because of our
presence in the beginning.

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:09.043
- There you go.

00:01:09.043 --> 00:01:09.876
- Yeah.

00:01:09.876 --> 00:01:11.191
- You sang the National Anthem.

00:01:11.191 --> 00:01:12.024
- Yes.

00:01:12.024 --> 00:01:13.890
- Which I usually don't touch.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:14.723
(laughing)

00:01:14.723 --> 00:01:17.570
And then, I sang
Centerfield in center field.

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:18.550
- Which is amazing.

00:01:18.550 --> 00:01:19.383
- It was fun.

00:01:19.383 --> 00:01:22.600
Anyway, and then we ended
up in the same area,

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:23.730
enjoyed the game together.

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:24.950
- Yeah and during the
game they go and put you

00:01:24.950 --> 00:01:25.830
in like the suites and whatever,

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:28.612
and it's like, I'm there for
the food and the drinks, John.

00:01:28.612 --> 00:01:29.445
(laughs)

00:01:29.445 --> 00:01:30.550
- Well, they had a lot of
hot dogs, I remember that.

00:01:30.550 --> 00:01:32.718
- Well, I'm Texan, so I'm not against it.

00:01:32.718 --> 00:01:33.926
(laughing)

00:01:33.926 --> 00:01:35.770
- But you and your kids,

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:38.860
you actually recorded an album
during the pandemic. Right?

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:42.423
- Yeah. This was a wonderful
idea that my wife, Julie had.

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:46.242
It was right as the lockdown started.

00:01:46.242 --> 00:01:47.075
- Yeah.

00:01:47.980 --> 00:01:49.990
- She thought, it started out this way,

00:01:49.990 --> 00:01:51.620
she said; "I want you to sing

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:53.167
'Have You Ever Seen the Rain'

00:01:53.167 --> 00:01:54.650
"and we'll post it."

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:57.050
And I was kind of grumpy
about the whole thing,

00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:59.640
'cause there's probably 40 or 50 versions

00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:01.670
of me on the web, you know.

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:02.503
- Yeah.

00:02:02.503 --> 00:02:03.540
- Singing, "Have You Ever Seen the Rain."

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:04.626
So why do we need this?

00:02:04.626 --> 00:02:06.283
- Yeah. It never gets old
John, so just so you know.

00:02:06.283 --> 00:02:07.140
- Uh huh?

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.228
- It doesn't get old. We
still love it every time.

00:02:09.228 --> 00:02:11.327
- Well, thank you for that.

00:02:11.327 --> 00:02:12.990
But it doesn't get old for me either

00:02:12.990 --> 00:02:14.850
in the right context but,

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:16.420
- Yeah.
what she was saying was,

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:19.380
I think it would be very
healing to people, you know,

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:22.370
uplifting, 'cause it is a hopeful song.

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:24.030
And I kinda

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:28.050
okay. And people, you know,
were generally in favor of that.

00:02:28.050 --> 00:02:28.883
So she,

00:02:28.883 --> 00:02:32.775
this is Julie, my wife kinda
kept it going, you know?

00:02:32.775 --> 00:02:33.608
- Yeah.

00:02:33.608 --> 00:02:35.650
- Like I said, I was kind
of an old grump about it

00:02:35.650 --> 00:02:37.760
except that I got to be with my kids.

00:02:37.760 --> 00:02:38.593
- I was gonna say

00:02:38.593 --> 00:02:40.280
that's the coolest part
to me in that photo,

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:41.510
I've seen that photo.

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:44.200
I get, I mean my kids are
only four and six right now,

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.460
but anytime, I mean, they love music.

00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:47.520
And they, you know,

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:49.370
have seen me on stage, they've
toured with me as well.

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:50.900
And when they get excited

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:52.900
and, like about the
things I get excited about

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:53.860
and things that inspire me,

00:02:53.860 --> 00:02:56.430
that's such a blessing as a parent.

00:02:56.430 --> 00:02:58.500
But you're performing a new song today,

00:02:58.500 --> 00:03:00.760
and can you tell me how
the song came about?

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:03.900
I heard that you've had
this title for years.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:06.870
- Yeah. I started keeping
a little notebook.

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:07.950
- Mm hmm.

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:12.060
- I mean, an official one in 1967.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:14.300
I realized I wanted to get, you know,

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:17.900
it all had been haphazard
up until then in my life.

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:22.370
And so I got a little book
and called it "Song titles."

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:24.590
And the very first thing

00:03:24.590 --> 00:03:26.640
that I ever wrote in
there was "Proud Mary"

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:28.740
which I didn't even know what it meant.

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:30.680
But anyway somewhere along the way,

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:34.037
more than 30 years ago I wrote the title,

00:03:34.037 --> 00:03:37.030
"Weeping In the Promised Land" Right?

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:38.950
And I looked at that and I went,

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:40.430
wow, that's pretty cool.

00:03:40.430 --> 00:03:42.260
Wow. That's sounds Biblical.

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:45.460
You know, and it sounds really sad.

00:03:45.460 --> 00:03:48.830
That's that's about all I
did with it at the time.

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:52.700
So basically our country
and the world were

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:56.120
very engulfed in this pandemic,

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:58.580
a health crisis all over the world.

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:02.810
And also about time again,

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:05.220
civil rights was coming to the fore

00:04:05.220 --> 00:04:07.910
in the Black Lives Matter
movement, it really got going.

00:04:07.910 --> 00:04:10.560
My daughter even went
down and was protesting.

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:12.591
She was in her senior
year of high school then.

00:04:12.591 --> 00:04:13.424
- Yeah.

00:04:14.924 --> 00:04:18.280
- So, somewhere around June or July

00:04:18.280 --> 00:04:20.900
that title, "Weeping In the Promised Land"

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:22.890
came across my consciousness again,

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:23.830
but this time it was, you know

00:04:23.830 --> 00:04:27.020
it was more like bam, you know, and I,

00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:29.550
Oh, wow.

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:31.560
'Cause now it meant, this.

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:32.393
- Absolutely.

00:04:32.393 --> 00:04:33.940
- You know?

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:35.400
And I have to admit,

00:04:35.400 --> 00:04:40.270
there was that kind of nanosecond
where I could see it all.

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:41.103
- Yeah.

00:04:41.103 --> 00:04:43.287
- You know, weeping and
here we are and it was like,

00:04:43.287 --> 00:04:47.800
"John, are you up to this?"
"Do you really want it?"

00:04:47.800 --> 00:04:49.603
If you take on this mission,

00:04:50.552 --> 00:04:52.440
- 100% in.
It's gotta be here.

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:55.180
And you can't let go until it's here.

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:57.410
And that's the course
of all writers you know,

00:04:57.410 --> 00:05:01.163
if you, and I use the
words, brutally honest.

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:03.810
And so I hung in there.

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:05.960
It was the hardest song I ever wrote.

00:05:05.960 --> 00:05:08.820
- But I think a lot of writers,
we have that happening,

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:09.780
especially that block,

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:11.920
especially when it's so
important to you it matters

00:05:11.920 --> 00:05:13.990
and you wanna do it justice.

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:15.490
Then that's a lot of pressure.

